# modules.cpp real-time clock interface

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.1 specifications. This document specifies the
implemented portable real-time clock interface and its PCF85063 controller
driver. mm.rtc, the PCF85063 driver, the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board provider,
and the board-smoke application exist. Clock behavior on hardware remains a
hardware-qualification requirement rather than a completed claim.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented mm.mcu
interface, including the I2C transport this driver uses. docs/modules-platforms.mdy
remains authoritative for SDK and board selection. docs/modules-mm.mdy remains
authoritative for the closed v1.2 manifest schema.

# Decision

A real-time clock is a device service layered above mm.mcu I2C. It is not a
time-zone engine, a formatter, an alarm scheduler, and not a policy module.

The intended dependency direction is:

Opening fence
portable application
        |
     mm.rtc
        |
selected board clock provider
        |
mm.rtc.pcf85063 (or another controller)
        |
I2C from mm.mcu
        |
selected MCU platform provider
Closing fence

An application using only portable clock behavior declares:

Opening fence
use: mm.rtc
Closing fence

It does not name a controller, vendor, I2C address, or provider module. The
selected physical board profile supplies those facts.

# Portable interface

mm.rtc is a platform interface with an allocation-free API for initialization,
calendar read and write, and a trust flag. Its fallback object reports
Unsupported.

The public shape is:

Opening fence
enum class Status { Ok, BadArgument, Unsupported,
                    NotInitialized, Busy, Timeout, TransportError };

struct DateTime {
    unsigned int year = 0;
    unsigned int month = 1;
    unsigned int day = 1;
    unsigned int weekday = 0;
    unsigned int hour = 0;
    unsigned int minute = 0;
    unsigned int second = 0;
};

class Clock {
public:
    virtual ~Clock() = default;

    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status initialize();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status read(DateTime& time, bool& trusted);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status write(const DateTime&);
};

void set_clock(Clock&);
[[nodiscard]] Clock& selected_clock();
Closing fence

DateTime is a plain calendar, not a count of seconds since an epoch. A
battery-backed clock counts calendar fields in hardware, and converting them
to an epoch here would pick a representation, an epoch, and an integer width
on behalf of callers that mostly want to print the fields back out. Nothing
in this interface needs `<ctime>`, which a freestanding lane may not have.

`year` is the full year rather than an offset from some base, because every
offset convention is a trap for someone. A part that stores two digits is the
provider's problem, not the caller's. `month` and `day` count from one.
`weekday` counts from zero, where zero is the part's own first day and mm.rtc
does not name which day that is.

`read` reports the time alongside a `trusted` flag. A clock that lost power
still answers: its registers hold whatever they held, and the reading is well
formed and wrong. The flag is an out-parameter rather than a field of DateTime
so that a caller has to name it, and rather than a Status so that a caller is
not pushed into treating a readable clock as a failure. A caller must decide
what to do about a reading it cannot trust, so the interface makes the clock
say so rather than hiding it behind a status a caller might treat as fatal.

Setting the time with `write` is what makes a later reading trusted.

The interrupt and alarm outputs a part may offer are deliberately absent, as
in mm.touch and mm.imu: mm.mcu has no callback registration. A caller that
needs an alarm polls `read` and bounds the loop itself.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board clock provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.rtc.

# Controller driver

mm.rtc.pcf85063 implements the NXP PCF85063 real-time clock over portable
mm.mcu I2C. It contains no vendor header and no board pin number: a board's
provider supplies the wiring and this module drives the protocol.

The driver receives immutable wiring:

Opening fence
struct Wiring {
    mm::mcu::I2cConfiguration i2c;
    unsigned int address;  // 0x51
};
Closing fence

Registers are eight-bit and the calendar is packed binary-coded decimal, so
every field crosses this boundary twice: once as BCD on the wire and once as
an ordinary number in mm.rtc's DateTime. The part stores two year digits, so
this driver fixes the century at 2000 and refuses a year outside it rather
than wrapping one silently.

Every register access is an mm.mcu `i2c_write_read` rather than a write and a
read, because the clock's address pointer must not be exposed to another
master between the two halves.

The `trusted` flag is read from the oscillator-stop bit in the same seconds
byte the time came from, rather than from a second transaction. This matches
the hardware: the PCF85063 latches the oscillator state alongside the time
registers.

Two things differ from the Waveshare reference driver for the
RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8. The reference reports oscillator stop by printing; here
it is the `trusted` flag mm.rtc requires, read from the same seconds byte the
time came from. The reference converts through `struct tm` and `mktime`;
mm.rtc has its own calendar so that a freestanding lane needs no `<ctime>`,
and the month and year offsets `tm` imposes are gone with it.

# Provider binding

mm.rtc carries the existing platform-interface marker. A concrete hardware
board binds it to one unique provider:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.rtc platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.rtc
Closing fence

The provider imports mm.rtc, the selected controller module, and mm.mcu. It
owns the fixed I2C wiring and clock address, constructs the controller, and
registers the Clock implementation from a retained static object. Existing
provider validation, board-over-SDK precedence, conditional closure
injection, and core platform-edge rules apply unchanged.

# Test strategy

Controller tests use a fake MCU Platform that records I2C operations. Tests
compare semantic register transcripts rather than merely checking that a
source file compiled.

Required host tests cover:

- initialization transcript;
- BCD packing and unpacking for every calendar field;
- read returns the calendar and the trusted flag from one transaction;
- write sets the calendar and makes a later reading trusted;
- oscillator-stop detection maps to trusted false;
- a year outside the 2000 century is rejected rather than wrapped;
- weekday passthrough without naming a day;
- transport errors propagated without continuing the register sequence;
- no clock or provider objects in an unrelated executable closure.

Target qualification builds one portable app through the real Pico external
CMake bridge, validates the ELF and UF2, and confirms exactly one clock and
MCU provider registration object. It then flashes through flash.sh.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add mm.mcu:i2c with host transcript tests.
2. Implemented: add mm.rtc with a host stand-in independent of any controller.
3. Implemented: add mm.rtc.pcf85063 with BCD, read, write, and trust tests.
4. Implemented: add the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board clock provider.
5. Implemented: add apps/board-smoke using mm.rtc alongside mm.display,
   mm.touch, and mm.imu.
6. Pending hardware: qualify reading, setting, oscillator-stop recovery, and
   power-loss behavior on the named Waveshare board.

# Acceptance criteria

- An app importing only mm.rtc links with exactly one selected clock provider
  and the provider's controller closure.
- The same app is unavailable on a board with no clock binding.
- An app not importing mm.rtc contains no clock provider or controller
  registration object.
- Controller host tests pass without Pico SDK or physical hardware.
- I2C register accesses cross the platform boundary as write-read
  transactions, not separate write and read calls.
- No controller source includes vendor or Pico SDK headers.
- No controller source includes `<ctime>`.
- A successful link or flash is not reported as a successful clock reading.

# What this does not settle

- PCF85063 is the first implemented controller. Its timer, alarm, and square-
  wave outputs are absent because mm.rtc has no callback registration and the
  board does not wire them.
- Time zones, daylight saving, formatting, and epoch conversion are policy
  above this module.
- Multi-clock selection and runtime clock discovery are not specified.
- A century beyond 2000 is not supported; the part stores two digits and the
  driver refuses rather than wraps.
- NTP synchronization, time sources other than a battery-backed crystal, and
  leap-second handling are outside this interface.
